WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.464 --> 00:00:09.478
What if the most useful AI workflow is not one that lets a model
control everything, but one that knows exactly where the model

2
00:00:09.478 --> 00:00:09.904
stops?

3
00:00:10.123 --> 00:00:19.763
That is the idea behind Jev Flow. Start with deterministic code:
explicit inputs, validated types, conditions, and branches.

4
00:00:19.941 --> 00:00:29.517
Given the same validated state and policy, the code takes the same
route. That makes a workflow testable, replayable, and easier to

5
00:00:29.517 --> 00:00:30.101
explain.

6
00:00:30.341 --> 00:00:39.688
The model judgment itself may still be uncertain. Deterministic AI
means the workflow controls uncertainty; it does not mean the

7
00:00:39.688 --> 00:00:42.901
model is infallible or magically repeatable.

8
00:00:43.104 --> 00:00:52.176
Jev is TypeSafe's System One model for bounded, typed judgments.
Instead of asking for free-form prose, ask one well-defined

9
00:00:52.176 --> 00:00:54.664
question about the supplied state.

10
00:00:54.864 --> 00:01:04.067
Jev can return a Choice, a Noul probability, or a Score. The flow
validates the shape, checks confidence and policy thresholds, and

11
00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:06.104
selects the permitted branch.

12
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:16.537
Treat that answer as evidence, not authority. It can be incomplete
or wrong. Code owns validation, routing, budgets, retries, and

13
00:01:16.537 --> 00:01:18.280
consequential effects.

14
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:27.338
Laya has a different role: it is an optional local adapter for
compatible models. You install Python and a compatible checkpoint

15
00:01:27.338 --> 00:01:31.560
yourself; Jev Flow does not bundle or download model weights.

16
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:40.972
Running locally changes privacy, latency, and compute tradeoffs,
but it does not remove model uncertainty. Hardware and model

17
00:01:40.972 --> 00:01:42.520
quality still matter.

18
00:01:42.741 --> 00:01:53.254
Other language models can draft or transform text. Jev can provide
a typed judgment, while deterministic nodes enforce rules. Give

19
00:01:53.254 --> 00:01:57.621
each system one clear job and label what actually ran.

20
00:01:57.824 --> 00:02:08.248
Now let's open the Jev Flow Studio. A flow is a graph: judgment,
switch, threshold gate, and output nodes connected by visible

21
00:02:08.248 --> 00:02:08.744
paths.

22
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:17.573
Here is our synthetic customer-support request. I am changing the
input field to a made-up duplicate-charge example, then entering

23
00:02:17.573 --> 00:02:19.560
fixed typed answers beside it.

24
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:27.995
Notice the banner: this is a synthetic scenario. The answers come
from the test fixture; the input text is not sent to Jev or

25
00:02:27.995 --> 00:02:29.840
interpreted by a live model.

26
00:02:30.064 --> 00:02:38.344
Run the preview, and the graph highlights the route. We can
inspect each step, see the branch, and replay the same fixture.

27
00:02:38.544 --> 00:02:47.037
That separation is practical: test the policy path before
connecting a provider, and keep the output trace beside the schema

28
00:02:47.037 --> 00:02:48.064
and conditions.

29
00:02:48.261 --> 00:02:57.345
After you explicitly configure Jev or another supported provider,
a live run can report its source, model, latency, and usage when

30
00:02:57.345 --> 00:02:59.581
those measurements are returned.

31
00:02:59.781 --> 00:03:09.505
A missing key, invalid answer, or unknown price should stay
visible as unavailable. A preview must never be described as a

32
00:03:09.505 --> 00:03:10.621
live judgment.

33
00:03:10.821 --> 00:03:22.341
The Compendium helps you explore combinations of patterns,
domains, variants, and thresholds. Its 388,080 figure counts

34
00:03:22.341 --> 00:03:27.181
generated configurations, not 388,080 model calls.

35
00:03:27.381 --> 00:03:36.066
In the Battle Arena, a fair comparison needs real configured
responses and observed measurements from both sides. Without them,

36
00:03:36.066 --> 00:03:37.981
there is no winner to claim.

37
00:03:38.181 --> 00:03:47.491
In the self-driving simulation, code creates each obstacle and
packages its type, lane, and distance with the car's lane and

38
00:03:47.491 --> 00:03:47.941
speed.

39
00:03:48.144 --> 00:03:57.590
Jev does not learn from each frame or inspect camera pixels here.
No OpenCV perception runs in this demo; real image understanding

40
00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.424
would require a separate vision system.

41
00:04:00.661 --> 00:04:10.017
The cart, chessboard, and other labs are bounded simulations or
local rules examples. They make behavior observable; they are not

42
00:04:10.017 --> 00:04:13.861
safety guarantees or proof of real-world performance.

43
00:04:14.064 --> 00:04:23.397
This is deterministic workflow design in practice: let models
supply structured evidence, and let ordinary code own the rules

44
00:04:23.397 --> 00:04:25.264
that turn it into action.

45
00:04:25.461 --> 00:04:34.954
Build from an example, edit a field, validate the graph, and
replay fixtures. Add a live provider only when you want a measured

46
00:04:34.954 --> 00:04:35.701
live call.

47
00:04:35.904 --> 00:04:44.797
Inspect every node, threshold, and trace. Keep uncertainty
visible, and keep human review available wherever your policy

48
00:04:44.797 --> 00:04:45.464
needs it.

49
00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:53.280
That is Jev Flow: a studio for workflows you can run, inspect,
test, and explain.
